---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import VerdictBadge from '../components/VerdictBadge.astro';
import { author, commissionKey, commissions, site } from '../config/site';
import { loadTopic, publicTopicNames } from '../lib/topics';
import { href } from '../lib/url';

const h2 = 'mt-10 mb-3 text-featured font-bold';
const p = 'mt-3 max-w-prose';
const list = 'mt-3 grid list-disc gap-2.5 pl-[1.2em] [&>li]:max-w-prose';
const names = new Set(publicTopicNames());
const label = (n: string) => loadTopic(n).label;
// One row per confidence band; the three columns are the same band applied to each verdict.
const rows = [
  { band: '90 to 100%', c: 0.95 },
  { band: '75 to 89%', c: 0.8 },
  { band: '60 to 74%', c: 0.65 },
  { band: 'under 60%', c: 0.5 },
];
---

<Base
  title={`About · ${site.name}`}
  description="What the Commonwealth of Reason is, which institutions publish its inquiries, and how to read a verdict."
>
  <header class="grid gap-4 border-y-4 border-double py-5">
    <div class="eyebrow text-muted-foreground">
      <a class="text-muted-foreground no-underline hover:underline" href={href('/')}>{site.name}</a> · {
        site.publicRecord
      }
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-display font-bold">About the Commonwealth</h1>
    <p class={`${p} text-muted-foreground`}>
      The {site.name} publishes through its commissions, bureaus, offices and society, each a YouTube channel with a remit.
      This site is their public record: every inquiry, with the sources it examined, the claims it pulled out of them, and
      the verdict on each claim with the evidence that decided it. The research is real, attributed and correctable, and no
      finding, seal or stamp claims more than the verdicts do. Motto: <em>{site.motto}</em>
    </p>
  </header>

  <h2 class={h2}>The institutions</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Each institution has a remit and publishes the inquiries that fall under it. A channel is linked once it exists; the
    others are named and waiting.
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-3">
    {
      Object.values(commissions).map((c) => (
        <li class="grid gap-1 rounded-md border bg-card p-4">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4">
            <a class="flex items-center gap-2 font-heading font-bold" href={href(`/commissions/${commissionKey(c)}/`)}>
              <img class="size-8" src={href(`/commissions/${commissionKey(c)}.png`)} alt="" width="256" height="256" />
              {c.name}
            </a>
            {c.youtube ? (
              <a href={c.youtube} rel="me" class="inline-block py-0.5 text-sm" title={c.fullName}>
                YouTube {c.handle}
              </a>
            ) : (
              <span class="eyebrow text-muted-foreground">Channel coming soon</span>
            )}
          </div>
          <span class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">{c.remit}</span>
          {c.topics.filter((n) => names.has(n)).length > 0 && (
            <span class="text-sm">
              Inquiries:{' '}
              {c.topics
                .filter((n) => names.has(n))
                .map((n, i) => (
                  <>
                    {i > 0 && ', '}
                    <a href={href(`/${n}/`)}>{label(n)}</a>
                  </>
                ))}
            </span>
          )}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 class={h2}>How an inquiry is built</h2>
  <ol class={list}>
    <li>
      <strong>Collect.</strong> YouTube videos with captions and blog posts, chosen to cover the pitches, the critics and
      the explainers on a question, not just one side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Extract.</strong> From each source, the four to twelve claims a viewer would walk away believing, each tied
      to a short verbatim quote. Claims are written once in canonical form, so the same idea from twenty videos is one claim
      with twenty sources behind it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Guess.</strong> Every new claim gets an AI judgment with a one-line reason. It counts for at most 50% confidence,
      however sure the model sounds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check.</strong> Studies, official data releases and reports go in a sources folder. Each is read against every
      claim, and a verdict is recorded only where a passage bears directly on the claim as worded, with a line citation. A
      study verdict replaces the AI guess when it is at least as confident.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Score.</strong> Evidence belongs to the claim, not the source. A verdict on a claim counts for every source
      that makes it, including ones added later. Each source's score comes from the verdicts on its claims.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class={h2} id="verdicts">How to read a verdict</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Each claim carries one of five verdicts. <em>Right</em>, <em>wrong</em> and <em>partly right</em> are judgments with a
    confidence from 0 to 100%. <em>Untestable</em> means framing, taste or a value judgment with no operational definition.{
      ' '
    }
    <em>Unknown</em> means testable, but the available evidence cannot settle it yet, including predictions.
  </p>
  <p class={p}>
    The label puts the confidence into words. The adverb comes from how sure the deciding evidence is; the colour comes
    from the verdict and fades as confidence drops. A <em>leans</em> label with a dashed border is an unverified AI guess
    or a weak study: it shows which way the evidence points, not a settled answer.
  </p>
  <section class="mt-4 max-w-wide overflow-x-auto" aria-label="Verdict labels" tabindex="0">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b text-left text-muted-foreground">
          <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Confidence</th>
          <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Judged right</th>
          <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Judged partly right</th>
          <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-medium">Judged wrong</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map((r) => (
            <tr class="border-b">
              <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground tabular-nums">{r.band}</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 pr-4">
                <VerdictBadge verdict="right" confidence={r.c} />
              </td>
              <td class="py-2.5 pr-4">
                <VerdictBadge verdict="partly" confidence={r.c} />
              </td>
              <td class="py-2.5">
                <VerdictBadge verdict="wrong" confidence={r.c} />
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
        <tr>
          <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">no judgment</td>
          <td class="py-2.5 pr-4" colspan="3">
            <VerdictBadge verdict="untestable" />
            <VerdictBadge verdict="unknown" />
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p class={`${p} text-sm text-muted-foreground`}>
    Confidence measures how strongly that evidence settles that claim, not how reputable the publisher is. A direct
    reading from an official release scores high. One econometric study scores lower, and lower still when a claim
    reaches beyond what the study measured. Hover a label to see the raw verdict and percentage.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>How sources are scored</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    A source's score averages its claims: right counts 1, partly right counts half, wrong counts minus 1, and the sign
    flips when the source argues against a claim. The average maps to four labels: <em>mostly wrong</em>,
    <em>mixed</em>, <em>mostly right</em> and <em>right</em>. Mixed means the source's correct and incorrect claims
    roughly cancel, or it only makes half-true ones.
  </p>
  <p class={p}>
    Novelty breaks ties. It rewards a source for claims that hold up and that few other sources make, weighted by how
    sure the verdict is. A video that repeats what twenty others say scores the same but ranks lower than one that adds
    something sound.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>What the Commonwealth does not do</h2>
  <ul class={list}>
    <li>
      It does not judge people or politics. Each verdict applies to one claim, in one period, against one body of
      evidence. Claims whose truth changes over time carry a period in brackets, and the same claim in another period is
      a different claim.
    </li>
    <li>
      It does not force a verdict. Predictions stay unknown until the data arrive. Value judgments stay untestable.
    </li>
    <li>
      It does not treat an AI guess as evidence. The guess is a starting point that a study can overturn, and the Recap
      tab of each inquiry lists every claim where that happened.
    </li>
    <li>It does not invent. Every citation, witness, authority and figure on this site is real and attributed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class={h2}>Who</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Published by <a href={author.links.GitHub}>{author.name}</a>, who writes about building things like this at{' '}
    <a href={author.blog.url}>{author.blog.name}</a>. The data, the agent briefs, the brand charter and this site are
    open at <a href={site.repo}>GitHub</a>. Every claim links back to its quote and every study verdict to the lines it
    rests on, so you can check the checking.
  </p>
  <p class={`${p} text-sm text-muted-foreground`}>
    <a href={href('/')}>Back to the Public Record</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
